  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-risk">What AI is actually changing for graphic designers</h2>

  <p>
    Set aside both extremes here too: "AI cannot touch real design taste, it is a creative field" and "AI
    has already replaced every design job." Neither survives contact with what is actually shifting task by
    task, and graphic design is genuinely one of the creative fields where this shift has moved fastest.
  </p>

  <p>
    Independent research on generative AI's labour-market impact, including the McKinsey Global Institute's
    2023 analysis of generative AI and the future of work, identifies creative and arts-related work as one
    of the categories seeing the sharpest acceleration in AI-driven automation of work activities, alongside
    STEM and business-professional roles. That research also finds these same categories among the ones
    still expected to add jobs overall, because generative AI is reshaping the mix of tasks inside a role
    faster than it is eliminating the role itself. For graphic design specifically, that plays out very
    concretely: tools like Canva Magic Studio, Adobe Firefly, and Midjourney-style generators already produce
    first-draft logo variations, background removal, resizing across formats, and stock-style illustration
    in seconds, work that used to consume a large share of a junior designer's early career.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What is shrinking</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Manual background removal, resizing, and repetitive format adaptation with no design ownership behind it.</li>
        <li>First-draft logo and icon generation, and templated social-poster work with no brand strategy attached.</li>
        <li>Generic stock-style illustration and moodboard assembly with no real curation judgment involved.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What is growing</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Brand strategy and the judgment to explain why a design choice serves a specific business goal, something current AI tools still cannot reliably reason through.</li>
        <li>Packaging, print, and physical-constraint design work, where real materials, dielines, and regulatory labelling rules matter, and a prompt does not know them.</li>
        <li>The verification layer: knowing which AI-generated option to keep, override, or reject for a specific brief.</li>
        <li>Client and stakeholder trust relationships that make a design actually get approved, produced, and used.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The practical takeaway for someone still deciding on this field: AI is making designers faster at the
    routine parts of the job, not making design judgment unnecessary. The staged, realistic path is to use
    AI-assisted tools now to compress first-draft and production time, then, as adoption matures over the
    next few years, move deliberately into the curation and strategy layer, catching a brand-inconsistent AI
    suggestion, defending a packaging decision, and using the freed-up time for more client-facing or
    specialist work instead of more routine template output.
  </p>

