    <a href="/blog/skills/graphic-design-vs-ui-ux-design-career-india/">graphic design vs UI/UX design career in India</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="freelance-vs-fulltime">Freelance vs full-time: which actually pays more</h2>

  <p>
    Graphic design is unusually well suited to freelance work compared with most other creative careers,
    because the deliverables, a logo file, a social kit, a packaging mock-up, are genuinely portable and do
    not need the designer physically present.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Full-time, in-house or agency</div>
      <ul>
        <li>More predictable income and a clearer growth ladder into senior, art-director, or creative-lead roles.</li>
        <li>Faster access to real brand-strategy exposure, since you sit inside real client or business meetings.</li>
        <li>Less control over project selection, but stronger structured feedback and mentorship early on.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Freelance or independent</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Genuinely global client access through platforms and direct outreach, with a low floor early on, roughly Rs 15,000-50,000 a month for most active freelancers.</li>
        <li>A real ceiling for designers who build a specific niche and repeat clients, well past what most salaried entry roles pay.</li>
        <li>Requires running an actual small business: pricing, contracts, collections, and client boundaries, not just design skill.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Neither path is a safer default. A full-time role gives structure and faster brand-strategy exposure
    early on; freelancing gives income ceiling and control faster, once a real niche and client base exist.
    Many strong designers move between the two: building brand judgment inside a job first, then taking that
    judgment independent once a specific niche and a few repeat clients are already proven.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who-fits">Who this path genuinely fits</h2>

